Study help

Flashcard Template Maker (index.html)
-------------------------------------
A single file web app. Open index.html in any modern browser. It works offline,
with no build step, no backend and no external libraries.

1. Build prompt: enter a subject, level, card count, card types and optional
   exam or notes, then copy the research prompt.
2. Paste the prompt into any LLM (Claude, ChatGPT, Gemini). It returns JSON
   (schema version 1, included in the prompt).
3. Import: paste or upload the JSON. Code fences and stray text are removed,
   the deck is validated, and problems are listed in plain language.
4. Cards: preview, flip, filter, untick, edit inline and export JSON.
5. Study order: topics sorted by prerequisites, with loops flagged, plus a
   printable one page study order sheet.
6. Print: A4 with cut lines and crop marks, 2 x 4 or 3 x 3 per page.
   Double-sided mode mirrors the back pages for duplex printing (flip on
   long edge). Single-sided fold mode prints front and back side by side.
7. Play: test yourself on screen with the loaded deck. Choose a topic,
   difficulty and length, then pick a challenge:
   - Quiz: multiple choice, up to four options per card.
   - Beat the clock: multiple choice against a 60, 90 or 120 second timer.
     Each wrong answer costs 5 seconds.
   - Sudden death: get through every chosen card on 3 lives.
   - Match up: pair cards with their answers, 5 to a board, with a speed bonus.
   - Type it: type the missing words of cloze cards, the term for a
     definition, or a short answer. Small typos are forgiven, and an
     "I was right" button overrides a strict mark.
   Answers in a row build a streak multiplier (x2, then x3). Results list the
   cards you missed, with a button to practise just those. Best scores are
   kept per deck and challenge.

   Voice mode (tick it on the Play tab) reads each question and its options
   aloud and lets you answer by speaking: say the option number ("two",
   "option 3") or the answer itself. Say "repeat" to hear it again, "skip"
   to pass, "next" to move on, or "I was right" to overrule a Type it mark.
   It is hands-free: after the feedback is read out, the next card starts on
   its own. Beat the clock pauses its timer while a question is being read.
   Match up is read out but played by clicking. Reading aloud works in all
   modern browsers. Listening needs Chrome, Edge or Safari and an internet
   connection, because the browser sends the audio to its speech service.
   The browser asks for microphone access the first time.

Setting up natural voices on Windows (no coding needed)
-------------------------------------------------------
Voice mode can use VoiceStudio, a free app that gives the flashcards natural,
human-sounding voices and understands your spoken answers. Everything runs on
your own computer. Setting it up takes about 15 minutes the first time.

STEP 1. Download the flashcards
  1. Open this link. The download starts by itself:
     https://github.com/EstusHealth/learning/archive/refs/heads/claude/funny-shannon-ukgbkl.zip
  2. Open your Downloads folder, right-click the ZIP file and choose
     "Extract All...", then click "Extract".
     (Do not just double-click into the ZIP. Windows needs the files
     extracted before they can run.)

STEP 2. Start the flashcards
  1. In the extracted folder, double-click "Start Flashcards".
     - If a blue box says "Windows protected your PC", click "More info",
       then "Run anyway".
  2. The first time, the Microsoft Store may open on a program called Python.
     Click "Get" or "Install", wait until it has finished, then double-click
     "Start Flashcards" again. You only need to do this once.
  3. A black window opens and the flashcards open in your browser.
     Leave the black window open while you play. Close it when you are done.

STEP 3. Install VoiceStudio
  1. Go to https://github.com/debpalash/VoiceStudio/releases/latest
  2. Under "Assets", click the file whose name ends in "win-x64.exe".
  3. Open the downloaded file to install it. If Windows says it protected
     your PC, click "More info", then "Run anyway".
  4. Open VoiceStudio. The first start can take a few minutes while it
     downloads its voice models.
  5. So it can understand speech: in VoiceStudio, open "Transcriptions" and
     download the recommended model if it offers one.

STEP 4. Turn on voice mode
  1. In the flashcards, load your deck (or click "Try sample"), then go to
     the "Play" tab.
  2. Tick "Voice mode" and set "Voice engine" to "VoiceStudio".
  3. A three-step checklist appears. Each step turns green by itself.
     When all three are green, pick a voice and press "Start".
  4. When the browser asks to use your microphone, click "Allow".

Every time after that: open VoiceStudio, then double-click "Start Flashcards".

If something goes wrong
  - The checklist says "You opened the file directly": close that tab and
    double-click "Start Flashcards" instead of "index.html".
  - It keeps saying "Waiting for VoiceStudio": make sure VoiceStudio is open
    and has finished starting. The page notices by itself within a few
    seconds.
  - It reads aloud but does not understand you: in VoiceStudio, open
    "Transcriptions" (or "Settings > Models > Dictation") and download the
    recommended model.
  - It hears itself and stops reading: turn off "Answer while it reads", or
    use headphones.
  - Nothing happens when you speak: check the microphone is allowed (the
    small icon at the left of the browser's address bar) and press "Mic".

VoiceStudio is a separate free program with its own licence (AGPL-3.0). Only
clone voices you have permission to use.

The last deck, settings and best scores are kept in the browser's local storage.
